{
  "analog": "Prepare, validate, review, and present temporary visual mesh edit controls on the USD preview. prepare (or open) stages controls without showing them and requires an expected effect and preserved features for every axis. validate generates actual-mesh before/after and displacement-overlay images for endpoints and combinations. Inspect every image, then review each axis and combined effects using the evidenceId; accept only useful, well-behaved edits, otherwise revise and prepare again. present requires accepted current evidence. Changes to the model, fields, parts or ranges require new validation. Pending work is reminded in context. Freeze the latest visible values into a recipe JSON before translating the edit back into exact build123d B-Rep CAD edits (cut_solid for cuts; parametric dimensions, lofts, or fillets for deformations while preserving exact joints); close cancels. Read mesh-edit-controls for intent and quality guidance. Persistent joint controls are authored in USD, independently of this workflow.",
  "cad": "---
name: cad-modeling
description: "Create, import, revise, or restore B-Rep CAD models with build123d Python. Publish and validate STEP and editable USD revisions."
---

# CAD modeling

Call \`cad_status\` for the bundled \`build123d\` Python executable, export helper, and joints kit paths. Run scripts through the ordinary shell tool (\`"<executable>" "<script>"\`), using its permissions and cancellation; do not build a separate execution service. If the CAD runtime is unavailable, report the setup action returned by the tool.

## Use checks at each stage

- **Explore drafts.** Keep testing alternatives while draft findings are open. Fix a defect immediately when it prevents the next experiment. Compare drafts against the requested silhouette, details and dimensions, and keep the best draft for comparison.
- **Refine the fit.** Once the parts have the intended shape, check exact surface clearances and per-part print orientation findings in \`RUMI_CHECKS\`. After each repair, verify that shape, wall thickness, and fit still meet the request. If an error count goes down but the design gets worse, return to the better draft and try a different construction.
- **Validate the chosen revision.** Resolve blocking geometry and fit errors before calling \`validation_mechanics\` or \`validation_accept\`. A draft with open findings can still be used for visual review.

## Start from the joints kit

For any model with more than one part, or with a wheel, hinge, slider, snap, bayonet, fastener, or gear, read [printable-joints](../printable-joints/SKILL.md) first and build with its kit (\`joints\` path from \`cad_status\`). It supplies millimetre \`build123d\` primitives, clean Boolean helpers, separate-print joint recipes, disassembly actions, and \`K.publish\`, which writes the STEP, B-Rep, and USD assembly in one call. Design moving parts for [separate printing and assembly](../printable-joints/SKILL.md#separate-printing-for-moving-parts); this is required, not a choice.

## Solid construction defaults

Use these defaults from the first modeling operation, including edits to existing models. They produce valid OpenCASCADE B-Rep solids and reduce print defects, though they do not certify print readiness.

- Work directly in **millimetres, Z up** using \`build123d\` (\`Box\`, \`Cylinder\`, \`Sphere\`, \`Cone\`, \`extrude\`, \`revolve\`, \`loft\`, \`sweep\`, and 2D sketches). Cursor snapshots, measurements, and highlight regions use the exact same model-local millimetre Z-up coordinates.
- Give every physical part a unique \`.label\` (its part name) and an optional \`.color\` (\`(r, g, b)\` tuple in \`0..1\`, \`'#rrggbb'\`, or \`build123d.Color\`). Each part must be a single closed \`Solid\` (not disconnected shells in one part).
- For multiple colours on one solid (displayed directly in the 3D viewer via USD \`GeomSubset\` and exported as painted faces in \`.3mf\`), call \`helper["paint_faces"](part, faces_or_predicate, color)\` (or \`K.paint_faces(...)\`). When a colour boundary crosses a smooth or large face (such as stripes or colour bands), call \`helper["split_faces"](part, *planes_or_faces)\` (or \`K.split_faces(...)\`) first to imprint crisp B-Rep face seams while keeping one valid closed solid. For procedural per-triangle colouring, use \`helper["paint_regions"](part, painter)\` (or \`K.paint_regions(...)\`) where \`painter(centroids_mm, normals)\` returns \`(T, 3)\`/\`(T, 4)\` RGBA values in \`0..1\` (\`NaN\` rows keep their prior colour). Never generate separate 2D software-rendered colour previews just to work around mesh colours.
- Fuse material with exact B-Rep Boolean union (\`a + b\`) and cut holes with Boolean difference (\`a - cutter\`). Give fused pieces meaningful overlap (at least 0.5 mm) and extend through-hole cutters past both outer faces. Keep separate mechanical parts and their clearances distinct.
- Build the primary solid before adding \`fillet\` or \`chamfer\` edges. Keep fillet and chamfer radii strictly smaller than adjacent wall thicknesses and edge lengths, and check \`BRepCheck\` status in \`RUMI_CHECKS\`.
- For symmetric objects, construct one half and mirror it cleanly (\`mirror(part, Plane.YZ)\` or \`K.mirror\`). Keep mating surfaces, bores, and resting faces analytically flat or cylindrical for accurate printing and clean print-bed orientation.
- Design each separate part with a flat resting face when practical so it prints cleanly without unnecessary supports. Inspect \`printOrientations\` in \`RUMI_CHECKS\` and \`report.json\` to check each part's bed-contact area and overhangs in its optimal print orientation rather than its assembled pose.

## Edit and publish

1. Read the current \`meshState\` context and the existing project files. Create another model folder only when the user explicitly requests or agrees to a separate model; clarify ambiguous intent before writing files. For edits and fixes, revise the script in \`source/\` and publish new revisions in the existing model folder. Preserve existing models. For an imported STEP or B-Rep asset, use \`helper["import_step"](path)\` or \`helper["import_brep"](path)\`. Legacy JavaScript previews are disabled by project isolation; migrate their actual geometry into \`build123d\` rather than approximating it from screenshots.
2. Write a reproducible Python script under \`source/\` (for example \`source/build_car.py\`). Run \`"<executable>" "source/build_car.py"\` through the shell tool.
3. Load the helper returned by \`cad_status\` with \`helper = runpy.run_path(helper_path)\` so importing never writes bytecode into the installed application. Call \`helper["publish"]("models/<name>", parts, assembly=...)\` (or \`K.publish(...)\` from the joints kit).
4. \`publish\` tessellates each B-Rep solid cleanly (<= 200,000 triangles and <= 64 MiB), saves an immutable revision under \`models/<name>/revisions/<uuid>/\` containing \`model.step\`, \`parts/<name>.brep\`, \`assembly.json\`, \`model.usda\`, and \`report.json\`, atomically updates \`models/<name>/model.usda\`, prints \`RUMI_MODEL <json>\`, and spawns \`cad/validate.py\` to print \`RUMI_CHECKS <json>\`.
5. Call \`viewer_load\` on \`models/<name>/model.usda\`. Saved models display independently of validation. Inspect \`RUMI_CHECKS\` (exact \`BRepExtrema\` part clearances, solid validity, and per-part print-orientation overhangs/bed contact) and \`meshState.validation\`. Use \`viewer_camera\`, \`viewer_cursor_snapshot\`, and \`viewer_highlight\` for visual inspection.
6. Before ending the turn, follow [Name Model Regions](../name-model-regions/SKILL.md) for the final revision you leave saved.

## Undo and revision restore

To revert to an earlier saved revision, call \`helper["restore_revision"]("models/<name>", "<revision-directory-name>")\` instead of rebuilding it. Identify the intended revision from its \`report.json\` and preview; do not guess from directory ordering. Restoration atomically restores the exact saved \`model.usda\`, retains all revisions, and returns the matching \`step\` and \`brep\` paths. Call \`viewer_load\` on \`models/<name>/model.usda\` afterward. Restoration leaves validation pending; run explicit validation before claiming acceptance.

## Translate frozen visual edit controls to B-Rep

When the person uses \`viewer_controls\` to explore a shape or planar cut visually on the USD preview and accepts their adjustment:

1. Freeze the controls with \`viewer_controls\` (\`action: freeze\`) to write the recipe JSON under \`artifacts/shape-controls/\`.
2. Load the recipe in your \`build123d\` script with \`recipe = helper["load_control_recipe"](recipe_path)\` and inspect \`summary = helper["summarize_control_recipe"](recipe)\`.
3. Apply the frozen input cleanly in exact B-Rep:
   - **Planar cut (\`operation == "cut"\`):** call \`part = helper["cut_solid"](part, recipe)\` on each target solid to perform an exact B-Rep half-space cut with planar capping and preserved cavity openings.
   - **Deformations (\`bulge\`, \`bend\`, \`twist\`, \`taper\`, \`surface\`, \`fields\`):** read the frozen values, target planes, profile station shifts, or displacement summary from \`summary\`, then update your parametric \`build123d\` dimensions, sketch points, lofts, sweeps, or fillets to realize that exact shape in clean B-Rep while preserving wall thickness, mating faces, and joint clearances.
4. Publish the updated B-Rep model, reload with \`viewer_load\`, and confirm \`RUMI_CHECKS\` and \`meshState\` before reporting the saved edit.

## Declare physical movement

Every multi-part or movable model requires a complete assembly description. Give each physical piece a unique part name and include retaining pins, caps, and axles. Disconnected solids cannot become one rigid piece by grouping them. Preserve requested features, connectors, names, controls, and movement ranges through repairs.

Use [Printable Joints](../printable-joints/SKILL.md) for kit-built assemblies. For custom assembly authoring, follow [USD assembly declarations](references/usd-assembly.md) for joint frames, retainers, disassembly actions, limits, and metadata.

## Movement controls

Use [control-widgets](../control-widgets/SKILL.md) to choose widgets and labels. Publish control layout changes in a model revision. Operating those controls afterward changes only the local preview pose, not saved geometry or validation input; pose changes need no freeze or save. Keep physical joint declarations and connectors intact when editing the UI.

Without UI declarations, revolute and prismatic joints generate sliders labeled by \`rumi:control\` or the child mesh name. To choose widgets, pass \`controls\` to \`K.publish\` (or set \`assembly["controls"]\` / \`rumi:motionControls\`). Each item has \`kind\`, \`label\`, and \`axes\`; each axis references an absolute joint prim path and may set \`step\`:

\`\`\`python
controls = [dict(kind="pad", label="Aim", axes=[
    dict(joint="/Model/Joints/Pan", step=1),
    dict(joint="/Model/Joints/Tilt", step=1),
])]
K.publish("models/turret", root=base, joints=joints, mode="separate",
          steps=steps, controls=controls)
\`\`\`

A slider or curve references one movable joint; a pad references two independent movements, horizontal then vertical. Joint limits supply axis ranges; reset returns to the saved pose at zero. Steps use degrees for rotation and millimetres for translation; defaults are 1 degree or 0.1 mm, capped to the range width. UI declarations cannot override limits or reset. Unspecified movements retain automatic sliders.

Joints sharing \`rumi:control\` share one value and must use matching units and overlapping limits. Reference that group once through any member joint. Repeated bindings, missing or fixed joints, unsupported widgets, and invalid steps withhold movement controls with a viewer diagnostic; geometry stays visible. The preview supports trees with up to 12 independent movements and preserves fixed descendants. Closed loops remain unsupported.

After publishing, inspect \`viewer_load\` feedback under \`design.motion\` and exercise each axis and reset. Invalid UI metadata does not change the physical validation result. Preview movement does not establish mechanical feasibility. Temporary mesh edits disable movement controls while keeping the panel visible. Reset the pose before source-bound cursor edits.

## Clearances and retention

Keep at least 0.3 mm between separate moving parts everywhere in the saved pose: radial gaps in bores, end-play, and door-to-frame gaps. \`RUMI_CHECKS\` measures exact B-Rep surface-to-surface clearance (\`BRepExtrema_DistShapeShape\`) and reports \`insufficient_clearance\` as an error below 0.2 mm, listing the pairs and exact gaps. Parts joined by a \`fixed\` joint may touch flush without error, while overlapping fixed volumes still fail.

Play is expected. Retention passes when the connector stops the part; the pivot may travel up to 2 mm and tilt up to 15°. Never tighten clearances to reduce drift.

Repair \`unretained_part\` and \`unstable_at_rest\` by adding or enlarging the capturing feature: a bayonet cap, snap flange, collar wider than the bore, or removable pin with locking cap. Do not tighten the fit. Repair \`insufficient_clearance\` by widening the listed gaps while keeping the capturing feature.

## Pointing and print export

Follow [Clarify Mesh Changes](../clarify-mesh-changes/SKILL.md) for captured pointing references. Recompute selections after a geometry change. Gaussian regions describe soft influence around a point.

Follow [FDM Design](../fdm-design/SKILL.md) for printability constraints and [Prepare Print Job](../prepare-print-job/SKILL.md) for plate arrangement and Bambu Studio handoff.

## Validate a finished revision

Every \`publish\` checks the new revision in a clean CAD Python subprocess and prints \`RUMI_CHECKS <json>\` with B-Rep solid validity, mesh/overhang findings (evaluated per part in its optimal print orientation), exact part-to-part clearance, any assembly declaration error, \`printOrientations\`, and \`dimensionsMm\`. Only findings with a nonzero count appear. Read the report after each publish and judge warnings against the intended design. If the status is \`unavailable\`, call \`cad_status\` once in this project and publish again.

The open viewer refreshes check status for the selected saved revision without accepting revisions or starting repair turns. Explicit tools reuse completed stages. When the design is ready and \`RUMI_CHECKS\` has no blocking errors, call \`validation_mechanics\` once with the model \`path\`. The host runs any missing assembly, geometry, and fit checks for that revision first and stops with their findings if one blocks. Otherwise it runs Bullet (up to ten minutes), testing whole-assembly contacts, retention, sampled movement, declared sequences, and disassembly \`actions\` / \`path\` steps with only the root held. Do not weaken settings, omit parts, narrow movement, or substitute a reduced assembly to obtain a pass; incomplete coverage remains unverified. Then call \`validation_accept\` with the \`path\` and \`fingerprint\` it returned. For moving assemblies, declare disassembly steps for every nonroot part; missing steps produce \`assembly_path_required\` and block acceptance while retaining motion findings. Only an \`accepted\` result supports an acceptance claim, and it does not certify slicing, deformation, strength, assembly accessibility, or print readiness. A printer volume that was never configured means printer fit is unchecked.

After any repair or placement change, publish again and repeat for the new revision; never report a previous revision's results as current. Validation never starts follow-up turns, so finish repairs in this turn. Preserve requested features, connectors, controls, and full movement ranges. If checks fail or cannot be resolved safely, report the remaining limitation briefly instead of claiming acceptance.
",
  "clarification": "---
name: clarify-mesh-changes
description: "Identify and highlight a feature from pointing or chat. Use the selected geometry to edit a feature or fit another part to it."
---

# Clarify mesh changes

Use ordinary language and the visible model to establish a shared reference. Use a highlight to visually ground region-specific edits and named parts. Follow clear requests, including undo and selections from choices you offered. Ask for clarification when the intended region or action is ambiguous; do not require a special confirmation phrase.

## Identify the region

For a planar cut, use the [live cut guide](../built-in-mesh-operations/SKILL.md#planar-cuts) as the visual reference.

When the person says or types a saved feature name or alias, pass it as \`viewer_highlight.region\`. Use the feature's stored location for the edit or explanation. When a name is unknown or ambiguous, ask which feature the person means. Use [name-model-regions](../name-model-regions/SKILL.md) to identify and explain useful features on the model.

For a new mark, use the cursor capture attached to that utterance or call \`viewer_cursor_snapshot\`. Check \`identity.design\` against the currently displayed model. Pass the returned \`mesh.path\` unchanged as \`viewer_highlight.meshPath\`. The STL contains the selected triangles in the saved model's coordinates, including during Movement. If the reason is \`named_regions\`, ask the person to say or type the feature name. If capture fails, becomes stale, or returns \`meshUnavailable\`, explain the issue and ask for a fresh pin on an editable model. For an empty Edge selection, ask them to pin nearer a boundary or switch to Surface.

## Use the selection for the edit

Whenever the requested edit depends on a selected region, identify its geometry before the first CAD edit. Read the STL at the captured or highlighted mesh path and use its triangles to drive the edit. If the reference is a saved surface name, resolve its triangles on the current model. Carry the same selection through follow-ups while it remains the intended target. Resolve it again if the model revision changes. The hit point identifies where the person clicked. The full selection determines the region’s extent.

When another part must fit the selection, derive its mating geometry from the selected surface or the 3D edge it shares with adjacent faces. Check clearance and overlap across the full interface, including edges and corners, before reporting the fit.

## Show the region

Read the current \`meshState\` context and inspect the selected model's source before choosing coordinates. Whenever you refer to a part such as a chin, jaw, cheek, eye, rim, or crease, first show that part with \`viewer_highlight\`, except when the continuous cut-line guide already shows the requested cut. Explain it as the teal highlighted area with a simple positional description. Do not assume the person knows anatomical or modelling terms. For several distinct parts, highlight and discuss one at a time; a combined highlight is appropriate only when the person is choosing the combined edit region. Named parts can help identify a region, but one mesh may contain many meaningful features. Describe left and right from the person's current view or use an unambiguous feature; do not silently switch between the wearer's and viewer's perspective.

For an editable model loaded with \`viewer_load\`, call \`viewer_highlight\` with \`design.path\` or \`selectedModel\` and the exact \`design.revision\` and \`design.url\` from the current \`meshState\` context. Give the region a short descriptive label. If you choose the geometry for a proposed region, write its surface overlay as ASCII or binary STL in the project outside the watched model directory, for example \`artifacts/highlights/region.stl\`. Supply its project-relative \`meshPath\`. Use model-local millimetres, Z up, with part transforms applied before viewer placement.

When the geometry supports a useful feature name, supply \`regionName\` and use a fresh path for each distinct overlay. Use the person's language and choose words they can reuse, such as “tail tip” or “inner corner of the tail.” Match the name to the triangles the overlay covers. If the person asks what they pointed at, show the captured selection before answering. Leave the name unset when the geometry only supports an unnamed patch.

Copy evaluated triangles from the source for exact parts. For steering-wheel spokes and wheel axles, put just those disconnected patches in one STL. For a band on a curved body, clip copies of the body faces to the band and export the resulting surface. The viewer draws the submitted triangles directly. A closed cutting volume would display its own faces. \`build123d\` parts are already in model-local millimetres, Z up; keep the editable source intact.

The tool waits for a matching receipt from the visible viewer. Only \`rendered\` confirms rendering; \`not_rendered\` or \`superseded\` does not. On failure, explain that the highlight is not confirmed, ask the person to open the preview, and retry. Never say “I have highlighted it” before a \`rendered\` result. Rendering still does not establish that the person saw or approved it. Use their reply in context, including corrections or a choice you offered. On \`stale_model\`, read the state again and resolve build errors. Ask for a fresh pin for a person's selection or recompute a proposed region against the new source. Highlights are temporary and clear when the source revision changes or another model loads. Imported STL objects do not support this tool.

## Establish the change

Ask one short question in the conversation about the uncertainty that matters, then wait for a spoken or typed reply. Do not use a questionnaire or require clicking an option. For an eye region, distinguish enlarging the openings from widening the surrounding material. Establish whether bigger means wider, taller, thicker, or more raised when the context does not settle it. Do not infer exact edit boundaries from the soft highlight.

When the person corrects the region, stop work based on the previous region, replace the overlay mesh, and clarify any remaining ambiguity. Keep their requested change in context. Do not make unrelated improvements while waiting. Hiding the highlight is a viewing preference, not approval. Confirmation of a region is not authorization for an unrelated change or a print.

When the requested change is clear, follow \`mesh-edit-controls\` if the person wants to adjust it themselves; prepare a faithful live binding without applying their edit first. For a direct edit, change the model source and inspect the resulting preview and measurements. Clear an obsolete reference with \`meshPath: ""\`, using the current model identity and an empty label. Explain changes using the person's feature names rather than coordinates or triangle indices. Only refer to labels or viewer controls that actually exist; do not invent hover labels.

Surface selection follows geometric boundaries such as creases and material seams. Check the captured mesh before naming an anatomical feature. For bilateral changes, inspect the model’s actual symmetry and highlight both intended regions; do not assume its symmetry axis or silently treat one side as both. Selection phrases change presentation only and never authorize an unrelated geometry edit.

## Example

For “make the eyes bigger,” highlight both eyes if both are intended. If the geometry leaves two plausible edits, ask whether to enlarge the openings or widen the surrounding material. If the requested effect is clear, proceed.
",
  "cursor": "Capture the pinned blue region or fresh hover hit with a PNG and model identity. Use a geometry cursor mode for pointing. In Named regions, ask the person to say or type the feature name. For a mesh selection, mesh.path is a project-relative STL in model-local millimetres, Z up, before placement. Pass mesh.path to viewer_highlight to repeat the triangle selection. For each new pointing request use its own voice capture or call this tool. See clarify-mesh-changes.",
  "fdm": "---
name: fdm-design
description: "Design model geometry for FDM printing, including bed fit, support, feature size, clearances, and strength."
---

# Design for FDM printing

Preserve the requested design while considering these constraints. They guide design; this skill does not run checks or certify print readiness.

## Core constraints

1. **Represent the intended solid.** Use explicit units, finite coordinates, and nondegenerate triangles. Each intended solid should have a closed, consistently oriented surface without self-intersections or nonmanifold connections. Slicer repair is not evidence that the intended geometry was preserved.
2. **Fit the usable printing region.** Check the proposed placement against the active nozzle's reachable region and exclusions. Reserve room for required supports, adhesion aids, and clearance. On dual-nozzle printers, do not substitute the combined envelope for either nozzle's usable region.
3. **Keep intended connections physical.** Features that must form one piece need a real material connection or an explicit assembly joint. Mere contact at a point or edge is not a reliable bond. Separate parts and color regions are allowed; shell count alone is not a defect.
4. **Plan support and bed attachment.** Choose an orientation with a viable first layer or an adhesion/support strategy. Regions above it need sufficient support from previous layers, feasible bridging, or generated supports. Temporary supports need a removal or dissolution route. Face angle alone does not establish support; a flat base and support-free printing are not universal requirements.
5. **Preserve printable features.** Size walls, posts, holes, gaps, and text for the selected nozzle, layer height, material, and wall generator. Thin features may disappear or widen during slicing. No fixed wall thickness, bridge length, or overhang angle applies to every configuration.
6. **Allow intended fits and motion.** Mating and moving parts need clearance or interference appropriate to the intended fit, material, orientation, and calibrated process. Do not assume nominally identical dimensions will fit. Keep at least 0.3 mm between separate moving parts; tighter fits fuse or bind. Use a small fit test when the result matters and calibration is unavailable.
7. **Design for intended use.** For functional parts, choose material, section thickness, connections, and orientation for expected loads and temperature exposure. Layer-dependent strength, shrinkage, and warping matter; successful slicing does not establish strength or dimensional accuracy.

## Sequence the work

1. **Get printer facts.** Read [inspect-printer](../inspect-printer/SKILL.md) for the selected model, nozzle diameter, single or dual mode, build volume and excluded areas. Without a selected printer, continue a visual draft and leave fit, feature size and clearance unverified rather than guessing defaults.
2. **Inspect the geometry you have.** Read the current \`meshState\` and the model's source. Record overall dimensions, the thinnest walls and posts, hole diameters, gap sizes, and which parts must move or stay separate. These numbers decide every constraint below; do not assess them from the render alone.
3. **Choose an orientation.** Pick the resting face for the first layer, then check what the choice costs: supported area, bridge spans, layer direction against expected load, and whether surface finish matters on the face touching the bed. Orientation is a design decision made now, not a slicer setting fixed later.
4. **Apply the constraints that the facts and the geometry disagree on.** Each numbered constraint above names a decision. When a requested design cannot meet one, say which and why, and offer the smallest change that resolves it before building.
5. **Validate before claiming readiness.** Run the model checks described in [cad-modeling](../cad-modeling/SKILL.md#validate-a-finished-revision), then slice and inspect the preview for unsupported regions, thin walls and layer time. Report what was checked and what remains unverified.

## Report the checks before recommending a change

When a design is near a printer, material, fit, or load limit, record the applicable measurements below before recommending a geometry change.

Record each applicable measurement or mark it \`unverified\` and name the missing fact.

| Ask | Cell | Answer |
|---|---|---|
| What can the nozzle actually place? | nozzle diameter × layers | e.g. \`0.4 mm → 0.8 mm min wall\` |
| Smallest wall, post or stroke in the model | measured value vs that cell | e.g. \`0.6 mm < 0.8 mm — too thin\` |
| Smallest hole or gap | measured value vs nozzle | e.g. \`3.0 mm — prints ~2.8 mm, drill after\` |
| Every moving pair | measured gap vs 0.3 mm | e.g. \`0.25 mm — binds, raise to 0.35\` |
| Overhangs and bridges | angle or span that needs support | e.g. \`55° overhang, 12 mm bridge\` |
| Orientation chosen | what it costs | e.g. \`flat — no support, 3 mm layer lines across load\` |
| Overall size | vs usable bed region | e.g. \`120×40×30 in a 220×220 bed\` |
| Facet held in service | material and load assumption | e.g. \`PLA, ~2 kg static — fine\` |

Then answer in this order: what must change, why each change follows from its row, and which rows stayed \`unverified\`. Do not lead with a change whose row you have not filled.

If the slice drops a thin feature, measure its extrusion width and adjust the feature or explain the loss. For undersized holes, consider an enlarged or teardrop opening and a finishing pass. Address corner lift with bed adhesion, geometry, or a material change. Repair open or degenerate geometry in the source and run validation again. Check orientation before splitting a model that exceeds the bed, and preserve the requested scale unless the user asks to change it.
",
  "highlight": "Show a saved named region or a project STL in teal on the editable model. Use the saved region for a request about its name or alias. Use the current displayedDesign identity and wait for rendered before describing the highlight as visible. See clarify-mesh-changes.",
  "instructions": "### Project type

This project is for designing and creating 3d prints.

### Project plugin

The primary plugin to enable that is print-studio, a Cordis plugin.

The plugin provides the ability to design, iteratively, the 3d model through its accompanying renderer.

The plugin provides the ability to prep and print the model to the user's personal 3D printer.

### Editable designs

Use \`build123d\` B-Rep CAD Python scripts for saved 3D models and Three.js for viewing. When someone asks to adjust a model themselves, read \`mesh-edit-controls\`, infer the desired geometric variation and preserved features, and choose or construct faithful bindings before exposing controls. Generate model-specific fields when appropriate; do not depend on prepared presets or silently substitute a completed edit. Call \`cad_status\`, then read the \`cad-modeling\` skill. Keep reproducible \`build123d\` scripts under \`source/\` and immutable STEP, B-Rep, and USD revisions under \`models/<name>/revisions/\`; publish \`models/<name>/model.usda\` with the provided helper. Create a separate model only after an explicit user request or agreement; otherwise edit the existing model and keep its revisions in the same model folder. Preserve existing models when adding an authorized model. The viewer discovers model folders and reloads completed USD exports automatically.

\`build123d\` works directly in **millimetres, Z up** — matching the viewer, measurements, cursor snapshots, and highlight regions with no unit scaling. Standard exported USD files use metres, Z up; the helper and viewer handle that conversion automatically. Keep named parts and parametric construction in your source scripts. Use the existing shell tool to run scripts with the \`cad_status\` Python executable (\`"<executable>" "<script>"\`); no GUI window is needed.

Call \`viewer_load\` with a USD path to bring it into view; use the current \`meshState\` context for models, selection, load errors and measurements. Self-contained static USD previews support at most 64 MiB and 200,000 triangles. Keep detailed STEP/B-Rep originals when explicitly making a lighter preview. Failed exports or loads retain the last successful preview and are not completed edits. Use \`viewer_transform\` for placement and \`viewer_camera\` for the viewing angle. Preview checks do not establish print readiness.

Executable \`model.js\`/\`model.mjs\` previews are disabled by project isolation. Preserve them when migrating their actual geometry into \`build123d\`. Do not modify the viewer or approximate an existing model from a screenshot.

### Movable prints

Build physical connectors and publish a complete assembly for multi-part or movable models, following \`cad-modeling\`, \`printable-joints\` and \`control-widgets\`. All moving physical parts must print separately and assemble afterward; follow the separate-printing requirement in \`printable-joints\` before designing connectors. Preserve rotation and sliding after assembly. Do not offer print-in-place, silently relabel captive geometry, or fix moving joints to bypass this requirement. Use a compatible \`printable-joints\` recipe for each wheel, hinge, slider, bayonet, snap, or gear before designing one by hand. Include every mesh, pin, cap, axle, connection, disassembly step, and movement control; preserve requested movement and full ranges during repairs. Keep at least 0.3 mm between separate moving parts and capture parts with assembled retainer geometry rather than tight fits; see \`cad-modeling\`. Use findings from each publish (\`RUMI_CHECKS\`) to guide iteration. When the design is ready, resolve blocking findings and follow the finished-revision workflow in \`cad-modeling\`. The open viewer refreshes checks for the selected saved revision automatically; explicit validation tools reuse completed stages. No automatic repair turns run. Models remain visible while validation and repair run. Read the matching \`meshState.validation\` report before claiming acceptance. Unsupported or incomplete checks remain unverified; a probe pass does not certify material strength or print readiness.

### Model references

Use the request's captured model selection as a default for an unnamed model, with explicit names and captured pointing references taking precedence. Later browsing does not redirect ongoing work. Clarify conflicting references before editing. Call \`viewer_working_model\` with a discovered entry before inspecting or editing it; update the declaration when changing targets and clear it before clarification. The green dot identifies your declared work independently of the selected model and clears when your agent stops. Read the current \`meshState\` context for both selection and working models. Use \`viewer_load\` only to change what the person sees.

The viewer renders every source through USD in Three.js. Keep editable \`build123d\` source scripts and STEP/B-Rep revisions; publish data-only USD from the confined modeling tools; STL imports are converted automatically. Declare assembly joints for movement controls; temporary edit controls use the supported USD preview deformation operations and freeze a recipe JSON that you translate back into exact B-Rep CAD edits.
",
  "interactive": "---
name: control-widgets
description: "Choose and declare sliders, two-axis pads, and scalar curve widgets for mesh edits or joint motion."
---

# Control widgets

Choose a widget after identifying what the person should be able to vary. Use [mesh-edit-controls](../mesh-edit-controls/SKILL.md) to design geometric edits and [cad-modeling](../cad-modeling/SKILL.md#movement-controls) to bind joint movement.

## Choose the widget

- \`slider\`: one independent numeric value.
- \`pad\`: two related spatial directions; the first axis is horizontal and the second vertical.
- \`curve\`: one numeric value adjusted by dragging the curve’s centered handle up or down. It does not provide draggable profile points or change the underlying mapping.

Prefer a slider unless the other gesture helps express the task. Name controls after their visible effect, using the person’s language. Expose implementation settings only when they help the person make the intended change. Labels do not create bindings. Do not promise numeric entry or units the widget and binding do not support.

## Declarations

A control has \`kind\`, \`label\`, and \`axes\`. Labels are nonempty and at most 80 characters. A slider or curve has one axis; a pad has two. A panel supports 1–12 controls. Bind each numeric value at most once.

The renderer’s axes have \`key\`, \`min\`, \`max\`, \`step\`, and \`reset\`. Values and ranges must be finite, \`min < max\`, and \`0 < step <= max - min\`. Initial and reset values must lie in range. Choose a useful range and a step small enough for deliberate adjustment. Reset restores the meaningful starting value.

Mesh-edit tools receive these axes directly. USD movement declarations instead reference joints; the viewer derives keys, limits, and reset values from those joints. Follow the relevant skill for the declaration format, delivery feedback, and save behavior.

Controls support keyboard input and per-control reset. Verify that each axis changes the intended feature and that reset restores its starting state. A rendered acknowledgment confirms delivery, not the quality of the interaction.
",
  "joints": "---
name: printable-joints
description: "Build separately printed assemblies with build123d CAD joint recipes for wheels, hinges, sliders, snaps, bayonets, threads, and gears."
---

# Printable joints

\`cad_status\` returns a \`joints\` path (\`cad/rumi_joints.py\`). Load it at the top of your \`build123d\` script:

\`\`\`python
import runpy, types
K = types.SimpleNamespace(**runpy.run_path(joints_path))
\`\`\`

The kit works directly in **millimetres, Z up** using exact OpenCASCADE B-Rep solids (\`build123d\`). Every recipe builds **separately printable** parts with real retaining hardware (quarter-turn bayonet caps, split snap barbs, removable pins, end-stop plugs, or collars), returns a \`RecipeResult\` with \`.parts\`, \`.joints\`, \`.steps\`, and \`.features\`, and registers its pieces so \`K.publish\` writes STEP, B-Rep, and self-contained ASCII USD with complete topologically ordered disassembly \`actions\` automatically.

Use a compatible recipe before designing a connector by hand.

See the [kit API](references/kit.md) for primitives, fillets/chamfers, exact Booleans, disassembly step helpers, and publishing.

## Separate printing for moving parts

Rumi requires every moving physical part to be printed separately and assembled afterward. Joint kind still describes movement after assembly: \`fixed\`, \`revolute\`, or \`prismatic\`. Do not offer print-in-place as an option or ask the user to choose a manufacturing mode. Publish movable assemblies with explicit \`mode='separate'\`; omitting the mode or using \`print-in-place\` is rejected. Single-piece static models need no manufacturing choice.

Design the assembly before choosing connectors. Provide accessible insertion paths, an assembly order, and separate pins, caps, or collars where needed. Explain it briefly: “The wheels and doors print separately, then attach with locking caps so they still spin and open.” Consider flat bed orientation, support removal, joint clearances, materials, strength, and repairability. Keep requested movement; never fuse a moving part or change it to a fixed joint to meet the requirement.

Every moving recipe in this kit is a separate-print design — there are no print-in-place moving joints. When building a custom connector, declare it with \`K.joint(...)\` (setting \`retainer\` on fixed retainers: \`'rigid'\`, \`'bayonet'\`, \`'threaded'\`, \`'snap'\`, or \`'friction'\`) and declare a disassembly step for every nonroot part using \`K.step_translate\`, \`K.step_twist\`, or \`K.step_snap\`.

Run the geometry, clearance, collision, retention, and movement workflow in [finished-revision validation](../cad-modeling/SKILL.md#validate-a-finished-revision). Bullet checks each nonroot part's disassembly \`actions\` (\`translate\`, \`rotate\` with optional helical \`pitchMm\`, and bounded elastic \`snap\` with \`maxInterferenceMm\`) plus full retention and motion probes with only the root held. Missing steps produce \`assembly_path_required\` and block acceptance. The \`separate\` declaration and plate placement alone are not proof that assembly is possible.

## Build order

1. Call \`K.start()\`, plan assembly after separate printing, and block out each physical part as a solid with its real outer size and position in millimetres.
2. Choose each joint's pivot and axis from those coordinates.
3. Call the recipe. It adds and cuts the mating features on both parts, creates any separate retainer hardware (caps, pins, collars, plugs), and records the joint records and disassembly \`actions\`. A recipe raises an error explaining what to move when a part does not reach the joint.
4. Add detail, fillets/chamfers, and colour (\`K.paint\` for whole parts; \`K.split_faces\` + \`K.paint_faces\` or \`K.paint_regions\` for multi-colour faces on one part). Build one side and \`K.mirror\` it for pairs.
5. Publish the assembled pose with \`K.publish("models/<name>", root=base, joints=joints, mode="separate", controls=controls)\`. \`K.publish\` auto-collects recipe-created parts, joints, topologically ordered disassembly steps, and badge features, prints \`RUMI_MODEL\` and \`RUMI_CHECKS\`, and updates \`model.usda\`. Follow [CAD modeling](../cad-modeling/SKILL.md#edit-and-publish) for region labels, \`viewer_load\`, and \`validation_mechanics\` + \`validation_accept\`.

Choose movement widgets through [cad-modeling](../cad-modeling/SKILL.md#movement-controls). Pass their declarations as \`controls\` to \`K.publish\`; joint geometry and limits remain the movement definition.

## Recipes are starting points

Each recipe is checked alone at its default size on a sample fixture; your whole model still has to pass its own \`RUMI_CHECKS\` and \`validation_mechanics\`. Resize a recipe through its options, and keep clearances (at least 0.3 mm for moving fits) and minimum walls intact when scaling a model up or down. When no recipe fits, model the connector with the kit and declare it with \`K.joint\` + \`K.step_*\`; copy the nearest recipe's proportions.

What the mechanics check needs, whichever way you build:

- Only the root part is held. Every other part must be caught by shape in every direction: a quarter-turn bayonet cap, a snap barb/ring wider than the bore, a removable keyed pin with locking cap, or an end-stop plug closing a slide. Friction, press fits, magnets, and glue hold nothing in the rigid check (\`retainer='friction'\` reports \`retainer_unverified_friction\`).
- Keep at least 0.3 mm between separate moving parts (\`RUMI_CHECKS\` uses exact \`BRepExtrema\` surface distance and flags \`< 0.2 mm\` as \`insufficient_clearance\`). Fixed mates may touch flush (\`0.0 mm\`) without error, while overlapping fixed volumes fail.
- Provide disassembly \`actions\` for every nonroot part (\`K.publish\` collects recipe steps automatically in topological removal order — caps/pins/plugs first, then moving parts).

## Catalog

All moving recipes print as separate pieces with real retainers and auto-generated disassembly \`actions\`. **verifiable** recipes pass \`cad/validate.py\` and \`rumi-bullet\` v2; **unverified** recipes rely on friction, magnets, or external metal hardware and stay marked unverified by design.

| Need | Recipe | Parts & retainer | Card |
|---|---|---|---|
| Wheel spinning on a stub axle (twist-lock cap) | \`K.wheel\` then \`K.bayonet_axle\` | 3 parts: axle + wheel + 90° bayonet cap | \`wheels.md\` |
| Wheel snapping onto a split stub axle | \`K.wheel\` then \`K.snap_axle\` | 2 parts: split barbed axle + counterbored wheel | \`wheels.md\` |
| Two wheels turning together on one axle | \`K.through_axle\` | 4 parts: body + axle/left wheel + keyed right wheel + keyed snap cap | \`wheels.md\` |
| Soft tyre on a rigid hub | \`K.tyre\` | 2 parts: pegged hub + snap-on TPU tyre | \`wheels.md\` |
| Door, lid, or flap (pin hinge) | \`K.pin_hinge\` (alias \`K.barrel_hinge\`) | 4 parts: parent/child knuckles + keyed pin + bayonet cap | \`rotation.md\` |
| Door that clips onto a post | \`K.snap_hinge\` | 2 parts: post + compliant C-clip leaf | \`rotation.md\` |
| Fold line in one flexible part | \`K.living_hinge\` | 1 part (PP/PETG/TPU, no rigid joint) | \`rotation.md\` |
| Tilting head or arm | \`K.ball_socket\` | 2 parts: slotted compliant socket + ball stem | \`rotation.md\` |
| Turntable, turret, spinner | \`K.turntable_plate\` then \`K.turntable\` | 3 parts: bayonet post + platter + 90° hub cap | \`rotation.md\` |
| Shaft turning inside a housing | \`K.bearing\` | 3 parts: bored housing + flanged rotor + snap retainer cap | \`rotation.md\` |
| Sliding carriage or drawer | \`K.dovetail_slide\`, \`K.tslot_rail\` | 3 parts: open-ended rail + slider + snap end-stop plug | \`sliders.md\` |
| Telescoping tube, piston | \`K.telescoping_tube\` | 3 parts: open chamber + flanged rod + twist-lock mouth collar | \`sliders.md\` |
| Latch or slider in a slot | \`K.pin_slot\` | 4 parts: slotted plate + bored block + stadium pin + keyed snap cap | \`sliders.md\` |
| Snap two parts together | \`K.snap_hook\` | 2 parts: chamfered window + cantilever hook | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Round snap-on lid | \`K.snap_lid\` | 2 parts: beaded rim + grooved keyed skirt | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Flat-pack wall into a base | \`K.tab_slot\` | 2 parts: slotted plate + barbed tabs | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Twist-lock cap | \`K.bayonet_lock\` | 2 parts: L-slot socket + lugged plug (\`retainer='bayonet'\`) | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Printed bolt and nut | \`K.bolt_nut\` | 2 parts: analytic B-Rep helical thread (\`retainer='threaded'\`) | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Push-fit pegs, dog-bone key, magnets, metal insert | \`K.press_pegs\`, \`K.dogbone_key\`, \`K.magnet_pockets\`, \`K.insert_boss\` | unverified: held by friction, magnetism, or hardware | \`fasteners.md\` |
| Meshing gears | \`K.gear_pair\` (blank: \`K.spur_gear\`) | 5 parts: base + 2 gears + 2 bayonet hub caps | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| Rack and pinion | \`K.rack_pinion\` | 6 parts: base + pinion + rack + 3 bayonet caps | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| One-way ratchet | \`K.ratchet\` | 5 parts: base + wheel + pawl + 2 bayonet caps | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| Cam lifting a follower | \`K.cam_follower\` | 5 parts: base + cam + follower + bayonet cam cap + keyed snap cap | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| Slider that clicks between positions | \`K.detent_slider\` | 4 parts: base + slider + 2 bayonet caps | \`mechanisms.md\` |
| One-piece spring | \`K.flexure_spring\` | 1 part (no rigid joint) | \`mechanisms.md\` |

Read the card before the first use of a recipe. Each family's file under \`references/\` beside this skill gives every recipe's call, what the parts must provide, the options worth changing, and how it prints and assembles.
",
  "load": "Select a CAD-exported USD in the Three.js viewer. Use models/<name>/model.usda; the viewer discovers these entries and reloads completed exports automatically. USD uses metres and Z up; the viewer converts to millimetres and Z up for measurements, placement and Gaussian regions. Use cad_status and the cad-modeling skill for B-Rep solid editing with build123d Python scripts. Keep STEP/B-Rep originals and revision exports. Self-contained static USD only, at most 64 MiB and 200,000 preview triangles. Preserve detailed originals when making a lighter preview. Executable .js/.mjs previews are disabled by project isolation; export USD instead. Reloading identical source bytes preserves staged controls and their review while requesting fresh renderer feedback. Changed source bytes invalidate them. Saved models display immediately; use validation_mechanics then validation_accept before claiming acceptance. Waits for renderer feedback for the exact source revision, placement and build volume. Returns load.status ready with design.report measurements and effective design.parameters, or an explicit build_failed, source_failed, viewer_hidden, timeout, superseded, cancelled or unavailable outcome. Only ready acknowledges a visible rendered model; timeout is not evidence of an unsupported model. Loading does not certify print readiness.",
  "meshEdits": "---
name: mesh-edit-controls
description: "Design, validate, and save live shape adjustments the user can try in the mesh viewer."
---

# Mesh edit controls

Use this skill when someone wants to adjust a shape themselves. A direct instruction such as “make it 20 mm wide” can be a source edit; a request to explore needs controls before applying the change. For existing joint movement, follow [cad-modeling](../cad-modeling/SKILL.md#movement-controls).

## Understand the edit

Read the current \`meshState\`, identify the intended model, and call \`viewer_working_model\`. Inspect the displayed revision’s source, dimensions, part positions, and construction. A named feature may be a region within one mesh or span several parts. Use [clarify-mesh-changes](../clarify-mesh-changes/SKILL.md) to ground the request visually. Ask one concrete question only when materially different interpretations remain.

Identify what should vary and what must stay fixed: openings, mating surfaces, wall surfaces, symmetry, connectors, and adjacent parts. Distinguish a profile change from local inflation and geometry edits from moving an existing part.

Choose a small set of useful adjustments. For each, define its geometric effect, affected region, neutral value, range, and protected features. Derive these choices from the request and current geometry; do not require prepared files, subject presets, or specific control names from the person. Source parameters help explain construction but are not automatically live bindings.

## Implement the adjustments

- Use a built-in operation only when its actual deformation matches the intended edit. Read [built-in-mesh-operations](../built-in-mesh-operations/SKILL.md) for bindings and regions.
- Use \`surface\` for a continuous side-view profile, radial band, groove, or planar patch when its geometry matches the request. Place profile stations from measured sections, choose the front direction, and pin stations around protected interfaces at zero travel rather than exposing those stations as controls. Link adjacent stations with the same displacement direction when a feature must move without changing its internal shape. Check crown continuity and the lower rim; a radial ring edit is not a whole-profile edit. Facets use target planes, not a smoother bulge. For an oriented hollow shell, supply the measured wall thickness so inner and outer faces target separate planes; avoid rim edges and verify the transition. Do not add a seated part to the edit merely to claim its fit is preserved.
- For other custom variations that preserve mesh connectivity, author [displacement fields](references/fields.md). Derive the mapping from the current construction, measured profile, or cross-sections. Evaluate source parameter changes on temporary copies when useful. Keep the generator in the project so it can be revised.
- New holes, count changes, and other unsupported live reconstruction need a specific explanation and an agreed alternative. Do not silently replace requested controls with static variants or a completed edit.

Check existing field files against the current revision and request before reusing them. Preserve the editable \`build123d\` source and STEP/B-Rep revisions. Executable JavaScript previews and \`operation: parameters\` are unavailable.

## Verify and present

State the expected geometric effect and preserved features for each axis. Prepare the controls through \`viewer_controls\`; preparation stages them without changing the person's preview. Validate the prepared controls to generate actual-mesh endpoint and combination comparisons. Inspect every before/after image, displacement overlay, and measurement against the expected effects. Check useful movement, direction, continuity, pinching, intersections, part fit, and both sides of shells. Measure planarity for flat-face requests and wall thickness for shell edits; displacement magnitude alone does not establish either. An inverted or collapsed triangle fails surface validation: reduce the range, protect the affected region, or repair the source, then validate again. Compare defects against the saved mesh. New intersecting triangle pairs fail even beside an existing defect; a lower total count does not excuse new pairs. Check wall thickness against the project’s minimum, not an invented threshold. If preserving a defective region requires it, move that region rigidly or keep it fixed. Repairing the saved source is a separate edit. A completed render is evidence, not a quality pass.

Record an assessment for each axis and the combinations. Revise ineffective, redundant, or disruptive bindings, then prepare and validate again. Present controls only after accepting the current evidence. The tool tracks pending work and invalidates evidence when the model or bindings change; do not substitute formula samples or self-authored tests for its comparisons.

Read [control-widgets](../control-widgets/SKILL.md) to choose widgets and labels. Field values start at zero. Use [built-in mesh operations](../built-in-mesh-operations/SKILL.md) for their neutral values. Briefly explain what changes and what stays fixed.

Only \`rendered\` confirms visibility. For \`viewer_hidden\`, bring the window forward; the viewer retries. For \`render_failed\`, fix the reported binding or model error. A timeout does not establish that the requested edit is unsupported.

## Revise unsuitable controls

When the person says controls are wrong or ineffective, reread this skill and inspect the binding. Distinguish delivery failure, a bad region or range, and an unsuitable deformation. Revise the adjustments or representation; relabeling the same operation does not fix it.

Preserve wanted unsaved adjustments; ask if keeping them is unclear. Close an explicitly rejected preview without baking it. Keep the saved revision intact while preparing replacement controls. Dissatisfaction does not authorize a static edit or saving.

## Save or cancel

Keep controls open during exploration. Satisfaction or moving to another task resolves the latest visible edit first. Do not use the original opening values in place of the person’s adjustments.

1. Freeze with \`viewer_controls\`, \`action: freeze\`, and the current control \`id\`. A failed freeze does not authorize saving stale values.
2. Call \`cad_status\` and inspect the frozen recipe JSON (\`helper["load_control_recipe"](recipePath)\` and \`helper["summarize_control_recipe"](recipe)\`). Translate that visual input back into exact \`build123d\` B-Rep edits in your \`source/\` script:
   - For \`operation: cut\`, call \`helper["cut_solid"](part, recipe)\` on the target solids for an exact B-Rep half-space cut with planar capping and preserved cavity openings.
   - For \`bulge\`, \`bend\`, \`twist\`, \`taper\`, \`surface\`, and \`fields\`, update your parametric \`build123d\` dimensions, sketches, lofts, sweeps, or fillets to match the frozen target dimensions/profiles cleanly in exact B-Rep while preserving mating faces, wall thickness, and exact joint clearances.
   Then publish and load the new revision using [CAD modeling](../cad-modeling/SKILL.md#edit-and-publish). Verify the published preview, \`RUMI_CHECKS\`, and that \`meshState\` has cleared the temporary edit and highlight before reporting a save.
3. For explicit cancel or revert, close the current \`id\` to restore the saved preview. If the source changes, reread state and reopen controls.

Failed previews preserve the last successful geometry and values. Failed execution leaves the original untouched on disk; report the error and allow reopening with the saved values. Unresolved controls are not a saved edit. A saved shape is not certified print-ready.
",
  "printJob": "---
name: prepare-print-job
description: Prepare a model for an adult to review, slice, and print in Bambu Studio.
---

# Prepare print job

Use [Print Studio setup](../print-studio-setup/SKILL.md) if the printer or slicer is unavailable.

1. Use [Inspect Printer](../inspect-printer/SKILL.md) to confirm the printer is reachable and resolve the applicable machine profile, nozzle mode, and loaded filament. Ask the adult which loaded AMS filament to use.
2. Read the current \`meshState\` before describing the preview. Use \`viewer_load\` for editable USD or \`mesh_open\` for an STL import. Reopening an STL adds a copy. Use \`viewer_transform\` for editable USD placement or \`mesh_transform\` for STL placement.
3. Check the placement against the profile’s usable region, including nozzle reach and exclusions. In the 3D preview, choose **Arrange for printing**, then **Open plate in Bambu Studio**.
4. In Bambu Studio, have the adult select the confirmed printer and filament, slice the plate, and review layer coverage, supports, and bed attachment. Use [FDM design](../fdm-design/SKILL.md) for geometry decisions. The adult sends the job from Bambu Studio.
",
  "printer": "---
name: inspect-printer
description: "Read printer status, loaded filament, and the applicable machine profile for printer-specific decisions."
---

# Inspect printer

Use observed printer facts for design constraints. A modeling request alone does not select a printer, nozzle mode, filament or build volume. Continue a visual draft when printer details are unavailable; leave print fit unverified.

## Retrieve the relevant facts

- Call \`print_studio_status\` for the configured printer, discovered devices, LAN reachability and \`slicer.profiles.root\`. A configured model is saved configuration; compare it with discovered identity before selecting a machine profile. If discovery cannot confirm it, label it configured rather than detected. Profile availability and setup \`ready\` do not establish that a particular job can print.
- Call \`print_studio_progress\` for live job state, temperatures, reported nozzle diameter, AMS trays and errors. Null, absent or incomplete fields mean unknown, not zero, empty or idle. Report when the reading was obtained; refresh live readings when answering a new status question or preparing a job. Older conversation results are historical.
- If the printer does not answer, say it is unreachable and its power state is unknown. A successful current report establishes that it is responding; failed discovery or a closed port cannot establish that it is powered off. Use \`print-studio-setup\` for missing configuration or rejected access. Do not repeatedly retry a failed probe without a relevant change.
- Read the applicable Bambu Studio JSON machine profile under the returned \`slicer.profiles.root\` through the file/shell tools. Match the confirmed or explicitly selected model, nozzle diameter and single/dual-nozzle mode; installed profiles alone do not identify the active selection. Follow named \`inherits\` profiles and apply child and explicit job overrides. Inspect \`printable_area\`, \`printable_height\`, \`bed_exclude_area\`, \`nozzle_diameter\`, nozzle-specific limits and applicable plate/process settings. Preserve the source paths and selected mode when reporting derived dimensions. If the selection or inheritance cannot be resolved, report the missing fact; ask only when it blocks the requested print decision.

Bambu Studio's documented CLI is for slicing and export, not a live-status command. Use the existing Print Studio tools for live telemetry; do not invent CLI flags or install a separate CLI to answer status questions. Keep access codes and tokens in the credential service, out of scripts, shell arguments and chat.

## Apply facts to the model

Pass \`buildVolume\` as three dimensions in millimetres to \`viewer_load\` or \`mesh_open\` only from an applicable profile or dimensions explicitly supplied by the user. Name that source. Never substitute a familiar printer size, a test-print preset or an existing viewer box for the selected printer's limits. The viewer box is a rectangular envelope; it does not encode excluded areas or different nozzle reach. Check those separately and use the slicer for final placement validation. An inherited viewer box is not evidence of printer fit; omitting \`buildVolume\` retains an existing box, so disclose an unverified retained envelope rather than relying on it.

Do not scale or split a design to satisfy an assumed envelope. Separate the measured model dimensions from the printer constraints. A material preset describes slicing settings, not a loaded spool. Report AMS material/color/remaining values as printer-reported; unavailable remaining amounts, external-spool details and physical plate identity stay unknown. Tray IDs may repeat across AMS units in the current tool output; do not guess a physical slot or nozzle mapping from an ambiguous ID. Ask the user to identify the intended filament when that choice matters.

Checking status does not authorize heating, motion, filament loading, calibration or printing. Preserve the existing confirmation flow for sending a prepared print. Present the relevant facts and uncertainties in plain language rather than dumping raw telemetry.
",
  "regionNaming": "---
name: name-model-regions
description: "Name model features from rendered images, model source and mesh measurements."
---

# Name model regions

Read the \`build123d\` construction source when available. Use \`viewer_camera\` and \`viewer_cursor_snapshot\` (or inspect saved preview captures) to view the model from two useful angles, opening close-ups when details are hard to distinguish.

Name the recognizable parts first. On creation or refresh, inspect each broad region for smaller features someone could point to or change independently. Repeat inside those features wherever the geometry supports another distinction. A handle might contain a grip with a thumb rest. A lip, recess or transition can be distinct even on one smooth surface. Keep uniform areas whole unless a landmark, a functional direction such as a vehicle's front, or the person's explicit request distinguishes specific sections. Use short names in the person's language and add familiar aliases.

Call \`viewer_analyze_surfaces\` with the saved USD \`path\` for its \`sourceHash\` and part paths. Pass an exact \`partPath\` array and \`sourceHash\` to list a part's connected surfaces. Follow \`nextOffset\` as needed. Match the images and source to the reported positions, bounds, and normals, then bind physical features to those triangles.

Save \`model.regions.json\` beside the final \`model.usda\` with that \`sourceHash\` and an ordered \`regions\` array. Give each region a stable lowercase hyphenated \`id\`, a \`name\` and a \`location\`. Bind each physical feature to its measured triangles. Write the completed JSON to a temporary file and rename it into place. For overlapping regions without a parent-child relationship, put the more specific region first.

For a \`surface\`, copy the part path and surface IDs from the tool.

\`\`\`json
{"id":"foot","name":"Foot","location":{"kind":"surface","parts":[{"partPath":["Cup","Body"],"surfaceIds":[6,9]}]}}
\`\`\`

Use multiple \`parts\` for a feature that spans meshes. For a patch within one smooth surface, request \`includeMesh: true\` and use the viewer-ordered STL at \`mesh.path\` to choose zero-based \`faceRanges\` of \`[start, endExclusive]\`. Omit both selectors for a whole part. Use a captured selection when the person supplied one.

Set each surface detail's \`parentId\` to its nearest named parent. Select a strict subset of that parent's triangles so zoom reveals a smaller highlight at each level.

For spatial features, use saved model-local millimetres with Z up.

- \`curve\` takes \`points\` along a path and \`closed: true\` for a loop.
- \`point\` takes a single XYZ \`point\`, such as a pivot.

## Verify the saved map

After saving, call \`viewer_analyze_surfaces\` with the model \`path\` and \`coverage: true\`. Read the uncovered triangle and area totals. Inspect gaps by passing \`partPath\` and follow \`nextOffset\` with the returned \`sourceHash\` and \`regionMapKey\`. Copy \`surfaceIds\` or \`faceRanges\` from the returned selection. When given \`faceRangesPath\`, read the ranges from that file. A connected surface can contain already named triangles.

Extend an existing name when it belongs on the omitted geometry, or add a name for a distinct feature. Leave uncertain patches unnamed and tell the person about significant gaps that remain. Rerun the audit after changing the map and use the final results when describing coverage.

Load the saved map with \`viewer_load\` and repair any reported region errors. Use \`viewer_highlight\` with a name and the current model identity to check the selection against neighboring features.
",
  "setup": "---
name: print-studio-setup
description: "Set up or repair Bambu Studio, printer discovery, and local printer access."
---

# Print Studio setup

Use this skill whenever a person asks to set up or repair the 3D printer, or when
\`print_studio_status\` reports something missing. Call \`print_studio_status\` first and
work from its \`nextSteps\`; do not guess.

## What Print Studio needs

- **Bambu Studio** — the slicer for the H2D.
- **A printer on the same network** — Print Studio finds its IP address and serial
  number; the LAN access code is fetched after the adult signs in to Bambu.

## If the slicer is missing

1. Tell the adult to press **Set up Bambu Studio** in **Plugins → Print Studio**.
2. Re-run \`print_studio_status\`; do not claim it is installed until the probe finds it.

Do not download or install applications yourself.

## Signing in does not require LAN mode

Print Studio uses local MQTT to read printer status. Bambu Cloud sign-in retrieves the LAN access code for that connection. A printer reporting \`DevConnect: cloud\` can still accept local access after sign-in. Keep the printer’s current connection mode when the adult uses Bambu Studio to print.
Ask the adult to use the **Sign in with Bambu** button. Never ask them to paste account credentials into chat.

## If the printer is not configured

1. Print Studio finds the printer and its IP/serial automatically.
2. The adult signs in once with their Bambu account in **Plugins → Print Studio**;
   Print Studio fetches the printer's access code from the bound-device list. Never
   ask for the account password or the access code in chat.
3. Re-run \`print_studio_status\` and confirm the access code was accepted before
   proceeding.

If the printer is on a different network or local access is blocked, say that Print Studio cannot read live status. The adult can check the printer connection in Bambu Studio.

## If the H2D profiles are missing

The H2D profiles ship inside Bambu Studio. If the probe finds none, the installed
Bambu Studio is too old for the H2D: ask the adult to update it, then re-run
\`print_studio_status\`.

## Reporting

Speak in plain language and name the one next step. Do not dump raw JSON at the person;
translate the status into "Bambu Studio is installed, the printer is not connected yet",
and so on. Use [Inspect Printer](../inspect-printer/SKILL.md) for status or [Prepare Print Job](../prepare-print-job/SKILL.md) to prepare a model for Bambu Studio.
",
  "shaping": "---
name: built-in-mesh-operations
description: "Bind a live bulge, bend, twist, taper, or planar cut to a selected mesh region."
---

# Built-in mesh operations

Use [mesh-edit-controls](../mesh-edit-controls/SKILL.md) to establish the intended change, verify it, and manage preview, save, or cancel. Use [control-widgets](../control-widgets/SKILL.md) for widget declarations. Built-in operations preview the existing geometry and materials locally; do not rebuild the CAD script while someone drags.

## Select the region

Read \`meshState\` and the \`build123d\` CAD source. Supply the current model’s exact \`path\`, \`revision\`, and \`url\`, a short region label, and exact mesh names in \`parts\`. An empty parts list selects every mesh in the region. Use \`viewer_highlight\` to establish a deformation region; cuts use the automatic cut-plane guide instead.

\`center\` is model-local XYZ millimetres. The local frame has N = \`direction\`, U = world-up cross N, and V = N cross U; near a vertical N, world-up is Y instead of Z. \`radii\` are extents along U, V, N. Choose a region that excludes protected features. Influence is compact and zero outside this ellipsoid. The ellipsoid limits deformations, not cuts.

These operations do not implement extrusion, curved cuts, remeshing, or beveling. For other variations that preserve connectivity, follow the field workflow in \`mesh-edit-controls\`; do not relabel an unrelated operation.

## Deformation values

Supply all six \`values\` in 0..1, but expose only axes useful for this edit. Declare axis ranges as min 0 and max 1.

- Bulge uses \`amount\`, \`angle\`, \`spread\`, \`peakX\`, \`peakY\`, and \`falloff\`.
- Bend, twist, and taper use all except \`angle\`. Their \`limit\` does not set an angle range.
- Cut uses \`amount\`, \`angle\`, and \`spread\`; supply .5 for the unused values.

For deformations, amount .5 leaves the geometry unchanged; larger values deform positively and smaller values negatively. For bulge, \`limit\` is maximum displacement in millimetres, no greater than the smallest radius. Spread .5 gives the supplied extents; peakX and peakY .5 center the effect. Angle .5 points outward. Falloff ranges from broad to concentrated; .35 is a useful starting value.

Start with zero deformation unless the person requested an initial change. Define reset values explicitly: amount, spread, angle, and peak normally reset to .5; choose falloff’s reset to match the intended starting profile. Reset affects only that control’s axes.

## Planar cuts

For a bottom cut, use \`operation: cut\`, \`direction: [0,0,1]\` to retain the upper side, and \`center\` at the proposed height. Amount moves the plane by \`(amount*2-1)*limit\` along direction; .5 is the proposed height. Angle tilts along V and spread leans along U, each spanning -60 to +60 degrees with .5 untilted. Positive tilt raises the plane toward positive V; positive lean raises it toward positive U. Set every radius at least as large as limit; radii do not clip the cut footprint. Select all parts that should share the edge.

Opening a cut clears prior mesh overlays and displays a continuous teal guide at the actual plane. A rendered cut receipt confirms the guide and controls. The guide disappears on freeze or close; other region highlights are rejected until the cut resolves. The viewer previews capped cross-sections; saving uses \`helper["cut_solid"](part, recipe)\` in \`build123d\` for an exact B-Rep half-space cut that preserves cavity openings.

Open controls before applying the requested cut. To restore material below an already cut edge, use the matching earlier revision.

## Change an open edit

\`meshState\` contains the last released values. Cursor snapshots and measurements during an edit do not describe a trustworthy deformed selection. Close the edit before changing its region; open a fresh session for a new direction, operation, or region. Follow \`mesh-edit-controls\` to preserve wanted adjustments or discard a rejected preview. Close restores the saved original and clears the region highlight.
",
  "stateToolAvailable": false,
  "surfaceAnalysis": "Inspect the saved USD for naming. Lists parts and connected surfaces with bounds, centres, normals and triangle counts. Can audit a saved region map for unnamed triangles. See name-model-regions.",
